Rules enforced in the database, not only the app:
• Reward approval runs inside one SECURITY DEFINER function that locks the campaign row (FOR UPDATE), checks remaining budget, writes balanced ledger lines, and increments spent_amount atomically.
• Clients have no INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE on any rewards table. They read their own rows through views.
• Withdrawal creation moves funds from user_available to a hold account in the ledger at request time; payment success moves it to user_paid_out; failure releases the hold with a reversing entry.
• Admin withdrawals above a configurable amount require two different approvers (approved_by <> second_approver).
3.7 Other modules (create with the same conventions)
• Gigs: gigs, proposals, contracts, milestones, deliverables, gig_reviews, disputes, dispute_evidence. Payment state references the licensed payment partner's transaction IDs; the platform stores references, not custodial balances.
• Network: connections (status, requested_by), follows, posts, post_media, comments, reactions, endorsements, recommendations, groups, group_members, conversations, conversation_members, messages, blocks, content_reports.
• HR: employees, employment_records, attendance_logs, leave_types, leave_requests, shifts, shift_assignments, payroll_runs, payroll_items, performance_reviews, hr_documents, announcements. Payroll tables store calculations and reports only.
• Ops: notifications, notification_preferences, reports, support_tickets, subscriptions, plans, payments, feature_flags, admin_actions, audit_logs (append-only, hash-chained: each row stores the hash of the previous row), data_deletion_requests, webhook_events (idempotent inbox).
• AI: ai_requests (module, model, prompt_hash, input refs, output JSON, validation result, latency, cost), match_results (job, candidate, score components, reasons, model version), scam_signals.
3.8 Row-Level Security patterns
-- Candidates see and edit only their own profile
create policy cand_self on core.candidate_profiles
  for all using (user_id = auth.uid()) with check (user_id = auth.uid());

-- Employers see candidates only if opted in AND through the controlled search view
create policy cand_discoverable on core.candidate_profiles for select using (
  visibility in ('public','recruiter_visible')
  and exists (select 1 from core.org_members m where m.user_id = auth.uid()
              and m.org_role in ('owner','admin','recruiter'))
);

-- Applications: candidate sees own; org members see applications to their org's jobs
create policy app_candidate on recruit.applications for select using (candidate_id = auth.uid());
create policy app_org on recruit.applications for select using (
  exists (select 1 from jobs.jobs j join core.org_members m on m.org_id = j.org_id
          where j.id = job_id and m.user_id = auth.uid())
);
Contact details are never exposed through the candidate view; they are released only through the release_contact RPC after a contact_requests row is approved by the candidate. Write pgTAP tests for each policy that prove cross-tenant access fails.
