Every function: validates input with the shared Zod schemas, derives the caller from the JWT (never from the body), rate-limits per user and per IP, writes an audit row for sensitive actions, and returns typed errors.
4.2 Auto Apply algorithm
1.  Cron enqueues eligible rules (active, not paused, under daily cap).
2.  For each rule, query newly published jobs since last_run_at that match every stored criterion.
3.  Skip excluded organisations, jobs already applied to, and jobs where match score is under the user's threshold.
4.  Require approved_resume_version_id; if the CV has changed since approval, skip and notify the user.
5.  Submit through application-submit with source = 'auto_apply', store the rule snapshot, notify the user immediately.
6.  Stop at the daily cap. Every submission appears in the user's log with a withdraw action.
4.3 Match engine (explainable)
Compute components independently and expose all of them:
score = 0.40*skills + 0.20*experience + 0.15*qualification + 0.15*location + 0.10*salary
skills        = weighted overlap of required/preferred skills (semantic similarity for near matches)
experience    = months relevant vs required, capped at 1.0
qualification = education level and certifications vs requirements
location      = distance or remote compatibility
salary        = overlap of candidate range and job range
Return {overall, components, missing_requirements[], suggestions[], model_version}. Excluded inputs: gender, age, religion, ethnicity, caste, marital status, disability, photo. Add an automated test that fails if any excluded field is referenced in the scoring code or features. Weights are configuration, versioned, and audited.
4.4 AI gateway rules
• All prompts live in versioned templates; every model response must pass a JSON-schema validator or is discarded and retried once.
• Resume parsing output is a proposal; the user confirms each field. Generation prompts forbid adding unsupported experience, and a post-check flags any claim not traceable to user-provided data.
• Scam detection combines rules (fees demanded, off-platform contact only, suspicious salary, mismatched domain) and a model score; high scores route a job to human review with the reason shown to the reviewer. It never silently deletes a listing.
• Nepali voice: speech-to-text, then voice-intent extracts {title, location, salary_min, employment_type, skills}; the UI shows the interpreted filters so the user can correct them.
• Cost controls: per-user daily quotas, caching by prompt hash, cheaper model for classification, circuit breaker on provider errors.
• No candidate personal data is sent to a model without a documented purpose; redact phone, email and ID numbers before prompts where not needed.
4.5 Rewards backend flow
start:      rewards-session checks eligibility + live campaign + remaining budget,
            reserves one completion's sponsor cost (reserved_amount += cost), issues signed task session (TTL = duration + buffer)
heartbeat:  every 5 s with monotonic counter; server accumulates watched_ms, rejects gaps, speed-ups, parallel sessions
question:   released only after watched_ms >= duration * min_watch_ratio; answer checked server-side
submit:     completion row = pending_review; risk score computed (device, velocity, IP/ASN, behaviour)
approve:    low risk auto-approves; medium goes to manual queue; high is rejected + fraud_flag
ledger:     on approval: debit campaign reserve, credit user_pending (hold period), credit platform_revenue (spread)
release:    after the hold period a job moves user_pending to user_available
expiry:     unfinished sessions expire and release their reservation
Enforce: one credited completion per user per campaign, daily and hourly velocity limits, device and account linkage checks, budget never overspent, and idempotency keys on every ledger post. Display only the campaigns that are actually funded and live; never present a daily target as a promise.
4.6 Withdrawals and payouts
• Phase A: manual review queue; admin exports approved payouts, pays through the partner's merchant portal, then enters the provider reference. Two approvers above the threshold.
• Phase B: automated payout API only after written partner approval. The worker is idempotent, signs requests, verifies callbacks, retries with backoff, and reconciles daily against partner statements.
• Destination account details are encrypted at rest (pgsodium / KMS key); only the payout worker can decrypt.
• Daily reconciliation job compares: sponsor funding received, campaign spent, user liabilities, platform revenue, payouts sent. Any mismatch raises an alert and freezes new payouts.
4.7 Notifications
Channels: in-app, push (FCM), email, SMS (Nepal gateways). Preference center per category, quiet hours, digest option. Transactional messages are separate from marketing and respect consent records. Job-alert fan-out is queue-based with deduplication.
4.8 Security and compliance engineering
• Auth: phone OTP with throttling, optional passkeys, session rotation, device list with revoke, step-up auth for admin and withdrawal actions.
• Storage: private buckets, signed URLs under 5 minutes, virus scanning on upload, file type and size validation, EXIF stripping.
• API hardening: strict CORS, CSP, rate limits, input size caps, request IDs, bot challenge on sign-up and rewards start.
• Secrets in the platform secret store; rotation schedule; no secrets in the client bundle.
• Privacy: consent ledger, export and deletion workflows (deletion anonymises but preserves ledger integrity), retention schedule per table, access logs for recruiter views of candidate data.
• Compliance gates before launch: legal review for recruitment, overseas employment, payroll, payouts, rewards and advertising; written sponsor agreements; payment partner approval; tax handling for payouts.
• Backups: point-in-time recovery, tested restore monthly, separate backup of ledger and audit logs.
4.9 Observability and operations
• Metrics: job-publish latency, search p95, application success rate, AI error and cost, queue depth, payout success rate, reward approval rate, fraud rate.
• Alerts: ledger imbalance (page immediately), budget overspend attempt, webhook failures, queue backlog, elevated auth failures.
• Runbooks for payout failure, fraud spike, data breach response and partner outage.
